WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:07.000
A taxi rank, says a new paper, is first of all an argument about whose turn it is.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.940
Most ranks leave it to a marshal, who calls each cab from memory.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:13.500
Some now give each driver a numbered ticket.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:17.319
This study asks which arguments the ticket actually settles,

00:00:17.519 --> 00:00:18.609
and where the rest go.

00:00:19.409 --> 00:00:24.471
We studied twenty-two ranks over a fourteen-week season.

00:00:24.471 --> 00:00:27.350
Thirteen had a ticket dispenser.

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:29.420
Nine had only a marshal.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:33.980
We had two coders watch every session, each keeping their own record.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:38.650
Between them, they sorted three hundred and ninety-seven disputes into seven categories.

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:44.900
Arguments over who arrived first fell from six point one per hundred boardings to point four.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.310
But the ranks argued just as often:

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:50.280
twelve point two with a ticket, twelve point six without.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:53.910
The arguments moved, mostly to drivers coming back from a break.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:57.040
Those disputes ran at four point two per hundred with a ticket,

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:58.500
and two point nine without.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:03.000
We think marshals were quietly letting drivers on a break keep their place.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:04.740
A ticket has no way to do that.

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:07.390
So a ticket settles the argument it names.

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:09.950
The rest move to wherever it has no say.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:15.190
We expected the dispenser to close the subject of boarding order entirely.

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:22.300
Instead there's a second argument sitting right behind the first one, waiting for its own instrument.
